.TH REAC-REPACER 8 "2026-07-29" "reac-repacer 0.2.3" "System Manager's Manual"
.SH NAME
reac-repacer \- de-jitter / re-pacing relay for a Roland REAC stream over Wi-Fi
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B reac-repacer
.B \-\-port
.I IN:OUT
.RB [ \-\-port
.IR IN:OUT " ...]"
.RB [ \-\-prefill\-ms
.IR N ]
.RB [ \-\-servo\-clamp\-ppm
.IR N ]
.RB [ \-\-pll ]
.RB [ \-\-forward\-only ]
.RB [ \-\-bcast\-only ]
.RB [ \-\-cpu
.IR N ]
.RB [ \-\-adapt ]
.RB [ \-\-adapt\-min\-ms
.IR N ]
.RB [ \-\-adapt\-max\-ms
.IR N ]
.RB [ \-\-adapt\-margin
.IR N ]
.RB [ \-\-reclaim ]
.RB [ \-\-no\-steady ]
.RB [ \-\-no\-warm\-start ]
.RB [ \-\-lockfile
.IR PATH ]
.br
.B reac-repacer
.RB { \-h | \-\-help }
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B reac-repacer
is a transparent Layer-2 relay that de-jitters a Roland REAC stream
(EtherType 0x8819) carried over a bursty Wi-Fi / WDS path. It sits between the
tunnel side (Wi-Fi, bursty) and the local REAC stagebox and re-emits the master
broadcast at a constant cadence, filling the gaps a clock-slave stagebox cannot
tolerate.
.PP
For each port the daemon buffers the incoming master broadcast for a few
milliseconds and re-emits it on a steady, free-running output clock derived from
the detected sample rate (44.1 / 48 / 96 kHz). Because the stagebox recovers its
word clock from the emit inter-frame interval, the output cadence is held free of
short-term wireless jitter: bursts are absorbed as buffer occupancy swings and
never reach the cadence; occupancy is used only as a slow, sub-ppm trim. The
return direction (stagebox to tunnel) is passed straight through, since the master
owns the clock and tolerates input jitter.
.PP
One daemon handles one or more REAC ports of a single mixer on one shared pacing
clock, so the stageboxes stay sample-phase-aligned with each other. The relay does
not decode the audio payload, reorder frames, or do VLAN tagging.
.PP
It is not byte-transparent. Every emitted frame has its 16-bit frame counter (bytes
14 and 15) re-stamped from the daemon's own monotonic sequence, so the output is
contiguous even where the input had holes; bytes 16 and 17 are read to tell a control
frame from an audio frame, so that control frames ride the cadence in sequence rather
than jumping ahead of it. A counter discontinuity observed downstream of the re-pacer
therefore did not come from the wire.
.PP
Gap concealment (on by default, see
.BR \-\-no\-plc )
goes further and emits a frame that never arrived: on ring underrun the last frame is
repeated under the next counter. A verbatim repeat is not used, because reusing a
counter reads as 65535 lost frames at the slave. Under a sustained underrun the
stagebox is therefore fed audio the master never sent, which is the intended trade but
means an unclicking box is not by itself proof that the link delivered every frame.
.PP
At least one
.B \-\-port
is required. There is no implicit default: invoked with no port, or with an
unknown flag, the daemon prints usage and exits non-zero rather than launching a
pacer on default interfaces.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-port " IN:OUT"
Define a REAC port as an IN:OUT interface pair (IN = tunnel side, OUT = stagebox
side). Repeat the option once per port. Required.
.IP
In UCI the same entry takes an optional third field,
.IR IN:OUT:etf_delta_us ,
which is the per-port ETF window. The init script consumes that field when it
installs the qdisc and passes only
.I IN:OUT
to the daemon, so the daemon itself never sees it.
.TP
.BI \-\-prefill\-ms " N"
Target buffer depth, in milliseconds, filled before emitting begins. Default 12.
.TP
.BI \-\-servo\-clamp\-ppm " N"
Drain-servo clock-bias clamp, in ppm; bounds how fast latency is reclaimed.
Default 700.
.TP
.B \-\-pll
Enable the glacial frequency-lock that converges the base period to null the
long-term occupancy drift. Default off.
.TP
.BR \-\-forward\-only ", " \-\-no\-return
De-jitter only the forward (IN to OUT) direction and do not run the return
pass-through thread. Used on the mixer side. The two spellings are the same flag.
Default off.
.TP
.B \-\-bcast\-only
Accept only the master broadcast frames. Default off.
.TP
.BI \-\-cpu " N"
Core to pin the real-time (SCHED_FIFO) pacing thread to. Default 3.
.TP
.B \-\-adapt
Auto-size the buffer to the observed burst depth. Default off.
.TP
.BI \-\-adapt\-min\-ms " N"
Floor latency, in milliseconds, when shrinking the adaptive window. Default 6.
.TP
.BI \-\-adapt\-max\-ms " N"
Ceiling latency, in milliseconds, when growing the adaptive window. Default 120.
.TP
.BI \-\-adapt\-margin " N"
Keep the occupancy low-water mark this many slots above zero (safety headroom).
Default 20.
.TP
.B \-\-reclaim
Actively shrink latency. This may drop frames and cause clicks; the default
behaviour is grow-only. Default off.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-steady
Disable the steady free-running output clock and restore the per-tick,
servo-paced cadence. The steady clock is on by default.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-warm\-start
Do not persist or restore the converged lock. Warm-start is on by default.
.TP
.BI \-\-lockfile " PATH"
Path of the warm-start state file. Default /etc/reac-repacer.lock.
.TP
.BI \-\-clock\-source " wifi|local|local\-in"
Where the emit clock's base reference comes from.
.B wifi
uses the occupancy PLL alone;
.B local
counts frames on the wired OUT port (the master crystal, exact, so Wi-Fi carries
data and never timing);
.B local\-in
counts them on the wired IN port, i.e. the mixer's rate. Default
.BR wifi ,
but the init always passes this flag, so on a router the directional profile decides
(AP side
.BR local ,
STA side
.BR local\-in ).
.TP
.B \-\-pace\-by\-downstream
Emit each frame as a response to a downstream frame arriving on the wired OUT port,
the way a real stagebox does, so the desk supplies phase as well as rate. AP /
master-side profile. Default off.
.TP
.BI \-\-clock\-margin\-ppm " N"
In the wired clock-source modes, the cumulative-rate change in ppm that re-applies the
emit period from the counted rate; it is also the PLL lock margin. Default 2; the
shipped UCI profile passes 8.
.TP
.BI \-\-detect\-ms " N"
Rate-detection / presence window in milliseconds. Default 60; the shipped UCI profile
passes 2000.
.TP
.BI \-\-detect\-confirm " N"
Consecutive detection windows that must agree before a sample-rate family change is
accepted. Default 2.
.TP
.BI \-\-mute\-ms " N"
Silence the audio for this many milliseconds across a rate-change transition, so the
re-lock is not audible. Default 250.
.TP
.B \-\-etf
Time egress with the kernel's SO_TXTIME / sch_etf rather than leaving frames at
sendmsg time with SCHED_FIFO wake jitter. Needs the etf qdisc on the OUT port, which
the init installs on the AP side. Default off.
.TP
.BI \-\-etf\-lead\-ms " N"
How far ahead of the target egress instant the pacer submits the frame to the kernel.
Default 4.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-plc
Disable gap concealment. With it disabled a ring underrun leaves the slot empty
instead of repeating the last frame under the next counter. Concealment is on by
default; see DESCRIPTION for what it costs.
.TP
.BI \-\-pll\-fgain " F"
Fraction of the measured rate error the PLL nulls per correction. Default 0.6.
.TP
.B \-\-pll\-pos\-min
Drive the PLL's position term from the tightest port's occupancy rather than the mean
depth across ports. Default off (mean depth).
.TP
.BI \-\-prio " N"
SCHED_FIFO priority of the pacing thread. Default 80.
.TP
.BI \-\-period\-ns " N"
Pin the emit period explicitly, in nanoseconds, and disable rate auto-detection.
Default 250000 (48 kHz), used only when auto-detection is off.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-auto\-rate
Disable rate auto-detection and hold the period at
.BR \-\-period\-ns .
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print the usage message to standard output and exit 0 without starting the
daemon.
.SS Diagnostic flags
These exist for A/B testing on a rig and must never ship enabled. They are not
reachable from UCI.
.TP
.B \-\-bypass
Relay frames straight through with no ring and no pacing.
.TP
.B \-\-ctrl\-bypass
Send control frames immediately instead of in cadence. This deliberately violates the
one-frame-per-slot rule and reproduces the heartbeat click artifact; it exists to
demonstrate the fault, not to be used.
.TP
.BI \-\-inject\-sine " SLOT[:FREQ]"
Overwrite a channel slot with a test tone.
.TP
.BI \-\-inject\-copy " SRC:DST"
Copy one channel slot over another.
.SH STEADY CLOCK AND WARM-START
With the steady free-running output clock (on by default), the emit period
free-runs at the detected base period and is not modulated per tick or per
occupancy swing. Short-term WDS bursts are absorbed as ring-depth swings and never
reach the output cadence, which removes the audible cadence jitter heard on
silences. Occupancy is used only as a glacial trim: the PLL, integrated over
seconds, nudges the base period sub-ppm to null the long-term rate error.
.PP
A glacial lock takes minutes to converge, and a cold start spends those minutes
audibly converging. Warm-start (on by default) persists the converged per-port
emit period to a small state file and, on startup, seeds the base period from it
so the daemon starts already locked. The lock is keyed per output interface, so a
router restores only the periods for the ports it is configured with, and only
when the saved rate label matches the rate detected at startup. A missing or
invalid file is tolerated: the daemon cold-starts exactly as it would without one.
.SH UBUS INTERFACE
When built with libubus support, the daemon registers a
.B reac_repacer
ubus object with two methods.
.TP
.B get
Return a live snapshot: the current target depth, base period, per-port occupancy
and counters, and whether an occupancy retarget walk is in progress.
.IP
.RS
.nf
ubus call reac_repacer get
.fi
.RE
.TP
.B set
Stage a hot retune of the reloadable parameters, applied on the next pacing tick
with no audio dropout. The hot parameters are
.IR prefill_ms ,
.IR adapt_min_ms ,
.IR adapt_max_ms ,
.IR adapt_margin
and
.IR servo_clamp_ppm .
A target-depth change does not flush the ring: the occupancy is walked to the new
depth by a small, bounded clock bias, so audio stays continuous and only the
latency moves.
.IP
.RS
.nf
ubus call reac_repacer set '{"prefill_ms":150}'
.fi
.RE
.PP
Structural parameters (the port / interface mapping and the pinned CPU) are
restart-only: a live change to them over ubus is rejected, not silently ignored.
.SH SIGNALS
.TP
.B SIGHUP
Re-read the hot parameters from the UCI configuration
.RB ( /etc/config/reac-repacer ,
section
.BR main )
and apply them through the same glitch-free path as a ubus
.B set
(omitted options keep their running value). This is how the procd
.B reload
trigger retunes a running daemon.
.TP
.BR SIGINT ", " SIGTERM
Shut the daemon down cleanly.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/reac-repacer.lock
Warm-start state file (overridable with
.BR \-\-lockfile ).
Line-oriented text, one record per output port, with
.RB ` # '
comments and blank lines ignored:
.IP
.RS
.nf
<out_iface> <emit_period_ns> <rate_label_hz> <unix_epoch>
.fi
.RE
.IP
For example:
.IP
.RS
.nf
lan1 124867 96000 1749150000
.fi
.RE
.TP
.I /etc/config/reac-repacer
UCI configuration read at startup and on
.BR SIGHUP .
There is a single
.B config repacer 'main'
section; it holds the global and hot parameters, and one
.B list port
entry per REAC zone. There are no separate per-port sections.
.IP
Not every UCI name reaches the daemon at launch:
.IR adapt_min_ms ,
.IR adapt_max_ms
and
.I adapt_margin
are read only on the SIGHUP / ubus path, so at startup the compiled defaults apply,
and there is no UCI name for
.BR \-\-adapt
at all.
.SH EXAMPLES
Three ports of one mixer with a 16 ms target depth:
.PP
.RS
.nf
reac-repacer \-\-port reactap.11:lan1 \-\-port reactap.12:lan2 \\
    \-\-port reactap.13:lan3 \-\-prefill\-ms 16
.fi
.RE
.PP
Mixer-side placement, de-jittering only the forward direction with an adaptive
window:
.PP
.RS
.nf
reac-repacer \-\-port reactap.11:lan1 \-\-forward\-only \-\-adapt
.fi
.RE
.PP
Retune the target depth of a running daemon over ubus, with no dropout:
.PP
.RS
.nf
ubus call reac_repacer set '{"prefill_ms":24}'
.fi
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR ubus (1),
.BR procd (8)
